00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:07.160
Hi again, everybody! 

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:08.160
Welcome to another video on conservation, and today

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:09.160
Welcome to another video on conservation, and today

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:10.160
Welcome to another video on conservation, and today

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:11.430
we're going to be talking about cleaning and
a little repair of feathers from taxidermy

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:16.200
birds. So, as you can see, and as you can
also see in the previous video in which we

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:18.630
talked about preventive conservation, there
are things that make the feathers not very

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:22.560
happy, such as, for example, light or dust;
all of these things cause us to have the problems

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:28.109
that we have in some of these feathers, which
really don't look too pretty to be able to

00:00:28.109 --> 00:00:31.439
be shown on display. So, I'm going to show
you some of the techniques that we use to

00:00:31.439 --> 00:00:33.870
repair these feathers.
For example, by simply using a brush, in many

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:37.879
cases, we can start to move the barbules,
which is the name these parts of the feathers

00:00:37.879 --> 00:00:42.149
have. And we slowly put them back together,
so that this bird, little by little, little

00:00:42.149 --> 00:00:50.660
by little, goes back to being the beautiful
heron that it was before it had so much dust

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:54.750
and was so long in the museum on display.
So, little by little, some of these tools

00:00:54.750 --> 00:01:01.572
help us unite these barbules. Why? Because
each one of them, what they have inside is

00:01:01.572 --> 00:01:15.360
a structure that are like hooks, then these
hooks get hooked, and we have... what we have

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:21.260
to do is join those parts. And many times,
also, we use our hands. There are times when

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:25.960
it is easier, depending on the feather, and
the location of that feather, little by little,

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:31.759
sometimes with our hands, we can work that
area, and we help ourselves with our tools,

00:01:31.759 --> 00:01:37.830
and slowly, we put that feather back in its
place. And this one is very bent, for example,

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:44.330
because who knows how long it has been in
that position, so it is comfortable in that

00:01:44.330 --> 00:01:50.270
position, and little by little, then, with
a lot of work and a lot of patience, we are

00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:57.940
going to put that feather, more or less where
it should be. And why don't we force it and

00:01:57.940 --> 00:02:04.490
make it go back in its place? Because again,
it has been in that position for a long time,

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:10.229
so today, for example, we can do this and,
in a few days, we can go back and continue

00:02:10.229 --> 00:02:12.050
working on this feather so that it can return
to its place.\

00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:16.560
But there are times when we have problems
that are a little bit worse. Like this area

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:21.610
over here, down here, which is kind of stained,
I don't know if you can see, and it also has

00:02:21.610 --> 00:02:25.593
like... the feathers are really not super,
super happy. So, we use, as an exception almost,

00:02:25.593 --> 00:02:29.720
a treatment that is liquid, right? We do all
the cleaning and everything\'85 we work with

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:33.141
the feathers dry. But now we are going to
use a mixture of ethanol, of alcohol, and

00:02:33.141 --> 00:02:48.970
distilled water. So, what we do is, we are
going to get a cotton swab wet, okay? And

00:02:48.970 --> 00:03:03.040
what we do next is dry it; we don't want it
to be super, super wet because we don't want

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:11.560
the feathers to be too wet. So, we are going
to go to the feather where we want to work,

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:22.900
for example, and we are going to put this
same absorbent paper on which we were demonstrating

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:35.240
the drying of that cotton swab, and we are
going to clean that part of the feather that

00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:39.630
has a stain that is not very pleasant, little
by little. We are going to leave this paper

00:03:39.630 --> 00:03:45.290
in place because we do not want that, if I
am wetting this particular feather, I do not

00:03:45.290 --> 00:03:54.500
want the feather next to or underneath it
to get wet. So, we slowly keep working and

00:03:54.500 --> 00:04:01.490
wetting these feathers very slowly, with a
lot of patience. And you can see that some

00:04:01.490 --> 00:04:10.560
of the stains are coming off the feather,
they are settling on the paper and little

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:19.790
by little then ... See? This one is all super
tangled, but with great care and attention,

00:04:19.790 --> 00:04:25.910
we are doing this so that this heron is as
beautiful as it was before. Little by little,

00:04:25.910 --> 00:04:31.780
check it out, this pen is a little better
than it was before and as I was saying, we

00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:37.910
have to work on these things every day little
by little so that the feather returns slowly

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:41.681
to its place.
This work is a big one, because look at the

00:04:41.681 --> 00:04:51.760
size of this bird, this beautiful heron, and
as you can see, I have a lot of work to continue

00:04:51.760 --> 00:05:02.710
doing, but I invite you not to forget to watch
the other videos that we have on repair of

00:05:02.710 --> 00:05:07.030
bone and bone cleaning, and on preventive
conservation. And of course, don't miss the

00:05:07.030 --> 00:05:09.720
rest of the videos in the series. 

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:10.720
Thank you!        

